<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#secretary-of-state-for-india">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Secretary of State for India</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as India Secretary, Indian Secretary, Secretary of State for India and Burma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Secretary of State for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was the British Cabinet minister in charge of the India Office in Whitehall, which oversaw relations with and the administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>. The post was created in 1858, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s rule ended and India, apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, was brought officially under the British Crown. After the reorganisation of 1937, which placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> under a new Burma Office, the same minister headed both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> as Secretary of State for India and Burma. The office was abolished in August 1947, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence-act-1947">Indian Independence Act</a> created the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> of India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Raj was governed from London by a <b>Secretary of State for India</b> and his India Council, and in India by a viceroy and governor-general, who ruled from Calcutta and, in summer, from Simla.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Appointed by the Crown, he headed the Government of India from Calcutta with an Executive Council whose members each ran a department, and he answered to the <b>Secretary of State for India</b> in London and through him to Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
